# Voice Infrastructure Architecture

## Overview

Little Nate's carrier-grade voice system supports live phone calls, in-app voice chat, and real-time coaching. The infrastructure uses a distributed worker pool model with tier-based routing and admission control for concurrent session management.

## Core Components

| Component | File | app.state Key | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| AdmissionController | `admission_controller.py` | `admission_controller` | Semaphore-based concurrent session limiter + queue |
| DistributedVoicePool | `distributed_voice_pool.py` | `voice_pool` | STT + TTS worker node management via Redis queues |
| VoiceRouter | `voice_router.py` | `voice_router` | Tier-based routing (cheap vs premium voice pipeline) |
| VoicePipelineOptimizer | `voice_pipeline_optimizer.py` | `voice_pipeline_optimizer` | Sentence-level TTS streaming for low-latency first-byte |

## Admission Controller

Controls how many concurrent voice sessions the system accepts.

| Parameter | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `DEFAULT_MAX_CONCURRENT` | 200 | Maximum simultaneous sessions per node |
| `SESSION_AFFINITY_TTL` | 3600s | Redis key TTL for session-to-node pinning |
| `QUEUE_POLL_INTERVAL` | 2.0s | How often queued sessions check for availability |

**Health methods:**
- `health()` → `{status, active, capacity, available}`
- `get_status()` → `{node_id, max_concurrent, active_sessions, available_slots, queue_depth, utilization_pct, accepting_new, total_served, total_rejected}`

**Rule**: If `utilization_pct > 90%`, the system is near capacity. If `accepting_new` is `false`, new sessions are being queued or rejected.

## Distributed Voice Pool

Manages STT (Speech-to-Text) and TTS (Text-to-Speech) worker nodes.

| Node Type | Default Engine | Hetzner Node |
|---|---|---|
| STT | `faster-whisper` (ARM) | LittleNate-1.X (37.27.244.80) |
| TTS | XTTS-v2 (GPU) | LittleNate-1.X (37.27.244.80) |

**Pool status:** `get_pool_status()` returns per-pool `{node_count, healthy_nodes, total_capacity, active_jobs, nodes[]}`.

**Health check interval:** 60 seconds per node. Nodes with `healthy: false` are excluded from job dispatch.

**Job TTLs:** STT jobs timeout at 30s, TTS jobs at 30s, results expire after 120s.

## Voice Router Tiers

| Tier | STT Provider | TTS Provider | Quality | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Standard | Pool → Whisper (Hetzner) → Azure | Pool → XTTS → Edge TTS → Workers AI | Good | $0 |
| Premium | Azure Realtime | Azure gpt-4o-mini-tts | Excellent | ~$0.01/min |

Premium tiers: `TOP_TIER`, `SOVEREIGN_CIRCLE`. All other tiers get the standard pipeline.

**Pool-first dispatch**: VoiceRouter checks `app.state.voice_pool` for healthy remote nodes before using local providers. See `voice-pool-wiring.mdc` for details.

## Voice Edge API Routes

The `nate-voice-edge` Cloudflare Worker proxies through these backend endpoints:

| Endpoint | Method | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `/api/voice/stt` | POST | Raw audio → VoiceRouter STT chain → transcript |
| `/api/voice/tts` | POST | JSON `{text, voice}` → VoiceRouter TTS chain → audio |
| `/api/voice/health` | GET | Voice pipeline + pool health status |

Router: `backend/app/routers/voice_edge_api.py`, registered in `main.py`.

**CRITICAL**: The `nate-voice-edge` worker must use `SOVEREIGN_API` (public `https://api.sovereignsanctuary.net`) for all backend calls. Never use WireGuard private IPs (`10.13.13.x`) — they are unreachable from Cloudflare Workers.

## Voice Pipeline Optimizer

Sentence-level chunking for streaming TTS — sends audio of the first sentence while generating the rest.

| Metric | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `avg_first_chunk_ms` | Latency to first audio byte |
| `avg_total_ms` | Total processing time |
| `total_requests` | Lifetime request count |

**Chunking constants:** `MIN_SENTENCE_LENGTH` = 20 chars, `MAX_BUFFER_CHARS` = 500 chars.

## Scaling Pattern — Cloudflare Realtime SFU + MoQ (Primary)

For carrier-grade load, three Cloudflare Realtime layers replace VPS-based media routing:

1. **SFU handles peer media plane** — audio/video tracks between users route through Cloudflare's global network, not the VPS
2. **MoQ handles Nate-to-many delivery** — one publish per member, infinite subscribers across 302 PoPs. No VPS fan-out.
3. **TURN handles NAT traversal** — 1 TB/month free relay for peers behind firewalls
4. **VPS handles intelligence only** — ODPE evaluation, C_emo, memory recall, voice generation (lightweight publishing)
5. **Unlimited concurrent sessions** — SFU + MoQ auto-scale globally, no per-node limits

See `cloudflare-realtime-webrtc.mdc` for credentials, API patterns, and MoQ namespace hierarchy.

